  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 225 AM EST Tuesday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1...A gradual warming trend is expected for the remainder of the week \nwith dry conditions continuing.\n\nA positively tilted trough continues to extend from the western \nAtlantic to Deep South with sfc high pressure over the region early \nthis morning. The surface high will remain in control through the \nrest of the week as upper ridging gradually builds over the area, \nand a closed low forms near the eastern Gulf Coast. With this, \nmostly sunny skies and a slow warming trend throughout the week are \nexpected. Highs today will be the coldest of the week, reaching ~60F \n(mid to upper 60s for the Eastern Shore). Temperatures will then \nrise a few degrees with highs reaching the upper 60s to ~70F Fri and \nthis weekend. Widespread temperatures below freezing inland are \nobserved early this morning and lows will continue to reach below \nfreezing for Wed and Thur morning. Lows will then be in the upper \n30s Fri and Sat.  \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Above average temperatures persist this weekend with \ndry conditions continuing on Saturday. There is a chance for rain on \nSunday, though confidence in rainfall is low (especially inland).\n\nThe upper ridge gradually breaks down by the end of the week, while \nthe Gulf Coast low slowly tracks NE over the weekend. The latest 00z \nglobal models now show the low a little closer to the area than \nprevious runs. The deterministic GFS remains an outlier with a \nstronger low moving up the coast into the local area, with the ECMWF \nand CMC move a weaker low just offshore. The differences in \ntrack and strength will impact rain chances. The latest \nensembles show a range of 0.0-0.50\" of rain this weekend, with \nhighest amounts along the coast and lowest amounts in the \npiedmont. Unfortunately, confidence in an appreciable wetting \nrain is extremely low west of I-95 at this time. Highs remain in\nthe 60s with lows in the 40s.",
